        "messages": [
          {
            "msg_id": "M11",
            "speaker_id": 455770,
            "reply_to": null,
            "text": "Okay team, based on the initial data from T1, I've drafted a new triage flow. The idea is to separate quick transactions from complex service requests right at the door. Please review the draft guide A2 and share your feedback. We need to make this practical for the floor.",
            "mentions": [
              73292,
              252240
            ],
            "artifact_refs": [
              "T1",
              "A2"
            ],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M12",
            "speaker_id": 73292,
            "reply_to": "M11",
            "text": "This looks like a solid start. The key will be the 'triage checklist'. For the 'quick transaction' rule, I suggest we define it as any single transaction that can be completed in under 3 minutes and doesn't require a manager's signature. That's a clear, objective rule for staff to follow.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "bulb",
                "count": 1
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M13",
            "speaker_id": 455770,
            "reply_to": "M12",
            "text": "Excellent point. A time-based rule is much clearer than a subjective list. Let's formalize that. Decision: Triage rule for 'quick' transactions is <3 minutes and no signature required.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [
              "D3"
            ],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "+1",
                "count": 2
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M14",
            "speaker_id": 252240,
            "reply_to": "M13",
            "text": "What about customers who have both? Like a quick deposit but also want to ask about a loan? That happens a lot.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M15",
            "speaker_id": 73292,
            "reply_to": "M14",
            "text": "That's a critical edge case. We could handle the quick transaction at the teller window and then direct them to the service desk for the loan query. Or offer a callback from the right team. We don't want the quick queue getting held up.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M16",
            "speaker_id": 455770,
            "reply_to": "M15",
            "text": "I prefer the callback option for anything complex like a loan query. It keeps the branch floor moving. Let's decide on this: For mixed cases, staff will handle the quick transaction and then offer the customer a scheduled callback from a specialist for the complex query. This will be our official process for handling these edge cases.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [
              "D4"
            ],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "white_check_mark",
