          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M2",
            "speaker_id": 511446,
            "reply_to": "M1",
            "text": "Great start. On the school side, it's usually an informal observation by the teaching assistant, followed by a chat with the class teacher. If concerns persist, we log it and might raise it at a staff meeting. Formal referrals are less common unless there's a clear safeguarding issue.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "+1",
                "count": 2
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M3",
            "speaker_id": 361403,
            "reply_to": "M1",
            "text": "From the community social work side, referrals typically come to us from multiple sources, including schools, but often after a significant event. We have a formal intake process, but the initial information from schools can be quite varied. Mapping this out in A1 will be key to finding the gaps.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [
              "A1"
            ],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M4",
